# draft
// FIRST DRAFT (M7): rewrite or sign off. Voice: docs/voice.md. Flags: docs/story-drafts.md.
Skögul has a heap of tally sticks on the table and is sorting them into two piles with the tip of her knife.
"Saga tallies. The family carves one for the grave: how they died, what they did, whose sword. The dead bring them up the path like a letter of introduction." # speaker: skogul
"Until now a tally's been nearly as good as a raven. Now somebody's forging them." # speaker: skogul
She holds one up to the rune-lens for you. Most of the runes are the sixteen every carver uses. One is not.
"That one's from the old alphabet. Nobody's carved it in three hundred years, except people copying from very old stones. That's one way you know." # speaker: skogul
"Some carve a rune backwards, the way you do when you copy from a reflection. Some get the carver's line wrong at the end. A real carver signs off 'reist rúnar', carved the runes. A forger writes 'reist mik', carved me, because that's what's on the swords." # speaker: skogul
"If a tally's forged, forget it. Every word of it. Judge by everything else." # speaker: skogul
* ["Who's forging them?"]
  "Somebody who can carve, and needs the silver, and thinks the dead deserve better than the truth." # speaker: skogul
  She pushes the forged pile toward you.
  "Which is most people, by the end." # speaker: skogul
* ["Can the dead forge their own?"]
  "The dead can barely hold a spoon." # speaker: skogul
- Down the table, the clerk is holding one of his own souls' tallies up to the light and frowning at it, and pretending he isn't.
-> END
